HUB.sections.find(s => s.id === 'compare').items.push(
{ q: `When should you use PropTypes instead of TypeScript, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Use the before-class checker for everything now.`,
  a: `TypeScript for all new code. PropTypes only in old JavaScript codebases on React ≤18 that can't adopt TS yet; note React 19 ignores them. For runtime validation of external data (API responses, forms) use zod or valibot, which TypeScript alone can't do.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between React.Children and Array.prototype.map in React?`,
  k: `Array map works on a normal list; React.Children knows the strange shapes children can come in.`,
  a: `<code>children</code> may be a single element, an array, a string, null, or nested arrays. <code>React.Children.map</code> handles all of these, flattens arrays, skips null/undefined, and assigns keys. <code>Array.prototype.map</code> only works if children is an actual array. React docs now mark the Children API as legacy-ish and prefer explicit props.` },
{ q: `When should you use React.Children instead of Array.prototype.map, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Use React.Children when you can't be sure what shape the children are.`,
  a: `React.Children (or <code>Children.toArray</code>) when transforming arbitrary <code>children</code> passed to your component. Array map for data arrays you own. Often better: accept an <code>items</code> prop or use composition/context instead of inspecting children.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between useRef and useState in React?`,
  k: `State is the picture on the wall (changing it redraws); a ref is a note in your pocket (changing it doesn't).`,
  a: `Both persist across renders. Updating state schedules a re-render and the new value is visible on the next render; it's for anything shown on screen. Mutating <code>ref.current</code> is synchronous, doesn't re-render, and shouldn't be read during render.` },
{ q: `When should you use useRef instead of useState, and vice versa?`,
  k: `If the screen needs to show it, use state. If it's a behind-the-scenes note, use a ref.`,
  a: `useState for values that affect output. useRef for DOM nodes, timer/interval ids, AbortControllers, previous values, "is mounted" flags, latest callback, and values read only in events/effects.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between React.lazy and Suspense for code splitting in React?`,
  k: `lazy says "fetch this toy later"; Suspense says "show this while we wait".`,
  a: `<code>React.lazy</code> defines a component loaded via dynamic import. <code>Suspense</code> is the boundary that displays a fallback while anything inside (lazy code, data) is pending. lazy needs Suspense; Suspense is also used without lazy.` },
{ q: `When should you use React.lazy instead of Suspense, and vice versa?`,
  k: `They're a team: you use them together.`,
  a: `Not either/or: use <code>lazy</code> to split heavy or route-level components, and place <code>Suspense</code> boundaries where a loading state makes sense in the UI. Use Suspense alone for Suspense-enabled data (use(), useSuspenseQuery, RSC streaming).` },
{ q: `What are the differences between React.memo and shouldComponentUpdate for performance optimization in React?`,
  k: `Two ways to say "skip redrawing": one for recipes, one for robots, and they answer the question the opposite way.`,
  a: `<code>React.memo</code> wraps a function component; its optional comparator returns <b>true when props are equal</b> (skip). <code>shouldComponentUpdate</code> is a class method returning <b>true when it should update</b>. sCU also sees next state; memo only props.` },
{ q: `When should you use React.memo instead of shouldComponentUpdate, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Recipes: memo. Robots: shouldComponentUpdate.`,
  a: `React.memo for function components. shouldComponentUpdate (or PureComponent) for class components with expensive renders. Avoid deep-equal comparisons in either; keep props stable instead.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between React.Children.map and React.Children.forEach in React?`,
  k: `map makes a new list; forEach just visits each child.`,
  a: `<code>Children.map(children, fn)</code> returns a new array of what fn returns. <code>Children.forEach(children, fn)</code> runs fn for side effects and returns nothing.` },
{ q: `When should you use React.Children.map instead of React.Children.forEach, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Need new children? map. Just counting or checking? forEach.`,
  a: `map to transform or wrap children (e.g. inject props with <code>cloneElement</code>). forEach to inspect, validate or count without producing output. <code>Children.count</code> and <code>Children.only</code> exist for common checks.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between controlled and uncontrolled components in React?`,
  k: `Controlled: React holds the puppet strings. Uncontrolled: the input does its own thing and you look later.`,
  a: `Controlled inputs store their value in React state (<code>value</code> + <code>onChange</code>): instant validation and derived UI, a re-render per keystroke. Uncontrolled inputs keep the value in the DOM (<code>defaultValue</code>, refs or FormData): less code and fewer renders, but harder to react to each change.` },
{ q: `When should you use controlled components over uncontrolled components, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Need to watch every letter? Controlled. Just need the answer at the end? Uncontrolled.`,
  a: `Controlled for instant validation, input masking, dependent fields, or disabling submit live. Uncontrolled for simple forms, file inputs, React 19 form Actions, and big forms via React Hook Form for performance.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between keys and refs in React?`,
  k: `Keys are name tags for kids in a line; refs are pockets to grab one thing.`,
  a: `<code>key</code> is a hint for reconciliation: it identifies elements among siblings so React preserves or resets state correctly. It isn't accessible as a prop. <code>ref</code> gives imperative access to a DOM node or a component's exposed handle (or holds a mutable value).` },
{ q: `When should you use keys instead of refs, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Lining kids up? Name tags. Need to grab one kid's hand? Pocket.`,
  a: `Keys for every rendered list and to intentionally reset a component. Refs for focus, scroll, measuring, media and imperative third-party APIs.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between PureComponent and Component in React?`,
  k: `Pure checks before redrawing; plain Component always redraws.`,
  a: `<code>Component</code> re-renders on every parent render/setState unless you implement <code>shouldComponentUpdate</code>. <code>PureComponent</code> implements it with shallow comparison of props and state.` },
{ q: `When should you use PureComponent over Component, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Pure when instructions are simple and stay the same; plain when they're always new anyway.`,
  a: `PureComponent for classes with immutable props/state and costly render. Component when props are new objects every time (the check wastes time) or when you rely on mutation (don't). For new code: function components.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between the key prop and the ref attribute in React?`,
  k: `Key helps React tell kids apart; ref lets you hold one kid's hand.`,
  a: `Same distinction as keys vs refs: <code>key</code> is consumed by React for list identity and never reaches the component. <code>ref</code> attaches to a DOM node or (React 19) is passed as a regular prop to function components, giving imperative access.` },
{ q: `When should you use the key prop instead of the ref attribute, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Lists need keys; grabbing needs refs.`,
  a: `key: lists and resetting state (<code>&lt;Editor key={docId} /&gt;</code>). ref: imperative actions like <code>focus()</code>, <code>scrollIntoView()</code>, measuring, canvas and video.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between forwardRef and useRef in React?`,
  k: `useRef makes a pocket; forwardRef used to pass someone else's pocket through a brick.`,
  a: `<code>useRef</code> creates a ref inside a component. <code>forwardRef</code> wrapped a component so a <b>parent's</b> ref could reach an inner DOM node. In React 19, function components receive <code>ref</code> as a normal prop, so forwardRef is no longer needed (and will be deprecated). <code>useImperativeHandle</code> customises what the ref exposes.`,
  c: `// React 19
function TextInput({ ref, ...props }) { return <input ref={ref} {...props} /> }
// parent
const inputRef = useRef(null)
<TextInput ref={inputRef} />` },
{ q: `When should you use forwardRef instead of useRef, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Making your own pocket: useRef. Letting a parent reach inside: pass ref through.`,
  a: `useRef whenever a component needs its own ref. forwardRef only on React 18 or earlier when a reusable component (Input, Button) must expose its DOM node to parents; on React 19 just accept <code>ref</code> as a prop.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between the children prop and the React.Children.map function in React?`,
  k: `children is what's inside the box; React.Children.map is a tool to go through it.`,
  a: `<code>children</code> is the prop holding whatever was nested in JSX; you can render it as-is. <code>React.Children.map</code> is a utility to iterate and transform that opaque value safely.` },
{ q: `When should you use the children prop instead of the React.Children.map function, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Just show what's inside? Use children. Need to change each piece? Use the tool.`,
  a: `Render <code>children</code> directly for layout and composition (most cases). Use Children.map only when the component must wrap or augment each child (e.g. adding separators). Prefer context or explicit props over cloning children.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between shallow rendering and full DOM rendering in Enzyme?`,
  k: `Shallow looks at one robot only; full builds the whole toy with all its parts.`,
  a: `Shallow rendering renders one level, children stay as placeholders: fast and isolated, but tests implementation details and misses integration bugs. Full DOM (<code>mount</code>) renders the whole tree in jsdom with lifecycle and effects.`,
  u: `Enzyme is obsolete. RTL always renders the full tree, which is the recommended approach.` },
{ q: `When should you use shallow rendering over full DOM rendering, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Today we always test the whole toy like a kid would.`,
  a: `In legacy Enzyme suites, shallow for isolated unit checks of a component's output, mount for interaction and integration. Modern answer: render fully with RTL and mock at the network boundary (MSW) instead of shallow rendering.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between Enzyme and React Testing Library for testing React components?`,
  k: `One checks the robot's insides, the other plays with the toy.`,
  a: `Enzyme: implementation-focused API (state, instance, shallow), class-component era, no React 18+ support. RTL: user-focused queries (<code>getByRole</code>), encourages accessible markup, resilient to refactors, works with any React version and with Vitest or Jest.` },
{ q: `When should you use Enzyme instead of React Testing Library, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Use the play-with-the-toy way.`,
  a: `RTL always for new tests. Enzyme only to keep an old suite running during migration on React ≤17.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between Redux Thunk and Redux Saga for handling async actions in Redux?`,
  k: `Thunk is a simple helper doing an errand; Saga is a manager watching everything and running complicated errands.`,
  a: `<b>Thunk</b>: dispatch functions with async/await; tiny, built into RTK, easy to learn; complex flows (cancellation, debouncing, races) get messy. <b>Saga</b>: generator-based effects, declarative, powerful orchestration (<code>takeLatest</code>, <code>race</code>, channels) and testable, but heavier and a steeper learning curve.` },
{ q: `When should you use Redux Thunk over Redux Saga, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Simple errands: thunk. A complicated chain of errands: saga.`,
  a: `Thunk (with createAsyncThunk) for most apps. Saga for complex long-running workflows, websockets, or when a codebase already uses it. For new code also consider RTK listener middleware (cancellation, debouncing) and RTK Query.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between localStorage and sessionStorage in HTML5?`,
  k: `localStorage is a diary that stays forever; sessionStorage is a notepad thrown away when the tab closes.`,
  a: `Both are synchronous key/value string stores (~5 MB, same-origin). <b>localStorage</b> persists until cleared and is shared across tabs of the origin (with a <code>storage</code> event). <b>sessionStorage</b> lasts for the tab's session and is per tab. Neither is safe for secrets: any script on the page (XSS) can read them.` },
{ q: `When should you use localStorage instead of sessionStorage, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Remember forever: diary. Only for now: notepad.`,
  a: `localStorage for preferences (theme, language), drafts, non-sensitive cache. sessionStorage for one-tab, temporary state such as a multi-step form or a redirect target. Neither for auth tokens; use httpOnly cookies. Use IndexedDB for large or structured data.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between a functional component and a class component in React?`,
  k: `Recipe brick vs robot brick.`,
  a: `Function components are plain functions using hooks; class components extend React.Component with lifecycle methods and <code>this</code>. Functions are shorter, compose logic via custom hooks, capture props per render, and support all new React features; classes are legacy except for error boundaries.` },
{ q: `When should you use a functional component over a class component, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Recipes for everything new.`,
  a: `Function components always for new code. Class only for error boundaries (or use a library) and existing legacy code.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between the debounce and throttle techniques in JavaScript?`,
  k: `Debounce: wait until the kid stops talking, then answer once. Throttle: answer at most once every few seconds, however much they talk.`,
  a: `<b>Debounce</b> delays a function until a pause of N ms since the last call: only the final call runs. <b>Throttle</b> runs the function at most once per N ms during continuous calls, giving regular updates.`,
  c: `function debounce(fn, ms) {
  let t
  return (...args) => { clearTimeout(t); t = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms) }
}
function throttle(fn, ms) {
  let last = 0
  return (...args) => { const now = Date.now(); if (now - last >= ms) { last = now; fn(...args) } }
}` },
{ q: `When should you use debounce instead of throttle, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Searching while typing: wait (debounce). Scrolling: update now and then (throttle).`,
  a: `Debounce for search-as-you-type, autosave, resize end, validation. Throttle for scroll position, mouse move, drag, analytics heartbeats. In React, keep the debounced function stable (useMemo/useRef) and cancel it on unmount; consider <code>useDeferredValue</code> for rendering-only slowness.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between var, let, and const in JavaScript?`,
  k: `var is an old box that leaks out of rooms; let is a box that stays in its room; const is a box with a lid glued on.`,
  a: `<code>var</code>: function-scoped, hoisted and initialised to undefined, can be redeclared, creates globals at top level. <code>let</code>: block-scoped, hoisted but in the temporal dead zone until declared, reassignable. <code>const</code>: like let but can't be reassigned (objects it points to can still be mutated).` },
{ q: `When should you use var instead of let or const, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Use const first, let if it must change, and leave var in the past.`,
  a: `Default to <code>const</code>; use <code>let</code> when you reassign (counters, accumulators). Avoid <code>var</code> in modern code; you'll only meet it in legacy scripts.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between event bubbling and event capturing in JavaScript?`,
  k: `Capturing: the message travels from the house roof down to the room. Bubbling: from the room back up to the roof.`,
  a: `DOM events travel in three phases: <b>capture</b> (window → target), <b>target</b>, <b>bubble</b> (target → window). Listeners run on bubble by default; pass <code>{ capture: true }</code> for capture. <code>stopPropagation()</code> stops travel. React supports both: <code>onClick</code> and <code>onClickCapture</code>.` },
{ q: `When should you use event bubbling instead of event capturing, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Usually listen on the way up. Listen on the way down only if you must hear it first.`,
  a: `Bubbling for normal handlers and <b>event delegation</b> (one listener on a parent for many children). Capturing when a parent must intercept before children (focus traps, analytics, closing menus on outside clicks before inner handlers run).` },
{ q: `What are the differences between deep copy and shallow copy in JavaScript?`,
  k: `Shallow copy copies the box but the toys inside are shared. Deep copy copies the box and every toy.`,
  a: `A <b>shallow copy</b> (<code>{...obj}</code>, <code>[...arr]</code>, <code>Object.assign</code>) copies top-level properties; nested objects are still shared references. A <b>deep copy</b> recursively copies everything: <code>structuredClone(obj)</code> (handles Dates, Maps, cycles; not functions or DOM nodes). <code>JSON.parse(JSON.stringify())</code> loses Dates, undefined and functions.` },
{ q: `When should you use deep copy instead of shallow copy, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Changing only the top? Shallow. Need to change deep inside without touching the original? Deep, or copy only the path you change.`,
  a: `In React, shallow copies along the path you update are the norm (copy each level you change), which keeps unchanged parts shared for cheap memo checks. Use Immer for deep updates. Deep copy (<code>structuredClone</code>) for fully independent snapshots, undo history, or data from untrusted sources you'll mutate.` },
);
